1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,872
Two AI agents.

2
00:00:02,152 --> 00:00:04,024
One cliff.

3
00:00:04,304 --> 00:00:09,968
Every time they step off the edge, they lose a hundred points and get thrown back to the start.

4
00:00:10,248 --> 00:00:12,288
Nobody told them where the cliff is.

5
00:00:12,568 --> 00:00:14,440
Nobody told them the rules.

6
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:16,592
They only get a score.

7
00:00:16,872 --> 00:00:23,112
And look at what happens: one of them learns the shortest path, right along the edge, and keeps falling in.

8
00:00:23,392 --> 00:00:27,256
The other one takes a longer, safer road, and scores higher.

9
00:00:27,536 --> 00:00:29,408
Same maths, almost.

10
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:31,560
One tiny difference.

11
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:36,976
By the end of this video you'll see exactly where it hides, in a single line of code.

12
00:00:37,256 --> 00:00:42,871
I'm going to be upfront: I built this whole thing with Claude Code, and I'll show you what I learned from reading it.

13
00:00:43,672 --> 00:00:50,128
Most machine learning you hear about is supervised: here are a million examples with the right answers, copy them.

14
00:00:50,408 --> 00:00:52,736
Reinforcement learning is different.

15
00:00:53,015 --> 00:00:54,887
There are no right answers.

16
00:00:55,167 --> 00:00:58,503
There's an agent, a world, and a reward signal.

17
00:00:58,783 --> 00:01:03,655
The agent tries something, sees what happens, and slowly figures out what works.

18
00:01:03,936 --> 00:01:12,384
That's how game-playing agents are trained, how robots learn to walk, and it's a big part of how language models get tuned to follow instructions.

19
00:01:12,664 --> 00:01:14,944
The tricky part is exploration.

20
00:01:15,224 --> 00:01:19,472
To find good actions, the agent has to sometimes do random things.

21
00:01:19,752 --> 00:01:22,176
But random things near a cliff are expensive.

22
00:01:22,456 --> 00:01:28,456
And that tension, between trying new stuff and not dying, is exactly what this demo shows.

23
00:01:29,256 --> 00:01:30,959
The world is a small grid.

24
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:35,823
S is the start, G is the goal, the skull cells are the cliff.

25
00:01:36,103 --> 00:01:39,968
Every step costs minus one, so wandering is punished.

26
00:01:40,248 --> 00:01:42,168
Reaching the goal pays fifty.

27
00:01:42,448 --> 00:01:46,264
The cliff costs minus a hundred and sends you back to the start.

28
00:01:46,544 --> 00:01:52,784
In the code that's literally one line of constants:  The agent's only knowledge is a table called Q.

29
00:01:53,064 --> 00:02:02,424
For every cell and each of the four moves, up, right, down, left, it stores a number: how good do I think this move is from here?

30
00:02:02,704 --> 00:02:04,528
It starts at all zeros.

31
00:02:04,808 --> 00:02:07,136
Learning is just nudging those numbers.

32
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:09,408
I also added three worlds.

33
00:02:09,688 --> 00:02:13,456
The classic Cliff walking, a Lava maze, and Two doors.

34
00:02:13,736 --> 00:02:15,608
We'll mostly use the cliff.

35
00:02:16,408 --> 00:02:18,280
Here's the whole learning loop.

36
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,319
I'll read it, because it's short enough to read..

37
00:02:21,599 --> 00:02:24,431
First, the Q table, all zeros.

38
00:02:24,711 --> 00:02:32,055
Then this pick function: with probability epsilon, a random move; otherwise the best move the table knows.

39
00:02:32,335 --> 00:02:36,031
That's exploration versus exploitation in one line.

40
00:02:36,311 --> 00:02:42,191
Epsilon is a slider in the sidebar, default point one, so one move in ten is random.

41
00:02:42,471 --> 00:02:44,343
Then the loop.

42
00:02:44,623 --> 00:02:46,495
Take a step.

43
00:02:46,775 --> 00:02:49,055
See the reward and the new cell.

44
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:51,207
Choose the next action.

45
00:02:51,487 --> 00:02:53,719
And now the key line, the target.

46
00:02:53,999 --> 00:03:00,671
The target is the reward I just got, plus gamma, the discount, times how good I think the next position is.

47
00:03:00,951 --> 00:03:03,039
And here's the one tiny difference.

48
00:03:03,319 --> 00:03:12,271
For SARSA, "how good is the next position" means: the value of the action I'm actually going to take next, which might be a random clumsy one.

49
00:03:12,551 --> 00:03:18,791
For Q-learning it means: the value of the best action available there, as if I'd never be clumsy.

50
00:03:19,071 --> 00:03:26,223
Same line, a ternary: the value of the next action I take, versus the maximum value in the next cell.

51
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:32,143
The last line nudges the table: move the old estimate a fraction alpha toward the target.

52
00:03:32,423 --> 00:03:33,239
That's it.

53
00:03:33,519 --> 00:03:35,391
That's the whole algorithm.

54
00:03:35,671 --> 00:03:37,615
Twelve lines and a table.

55
00:03:37,895 --> 00:03:46,079
I also snapshot the table at about forty points, spaced on a log scale, so the UI can let you scrub through training without retraining.

56
00:03:46,359 --> 00:03:51,423
That was a small design choice I liked: training runs once, scrubbing is instant.

57
00:03:51,703 --> 00:03:59,071
One more helper: greedy path follows the best move from the start until it reaches the goal, and that's the blue line you'll see.

58
00:03:59,871 --> 00:04:01,743
Okay, live.

59
00:04:02,023 --> 00:04:05,887
Cliff walking, a thousand episodes, epsilon point one.

60
00:04:06,167 --> 00:04:08,807
Both agents train when the page loads.

61
00:04:09,087 --> 00:04:11,799
I'll drag the scrub slider back to the beginning.

62
00:04:12,079 --> 00:04:17,815
Episode one: the heatmap is empty, there are no arrows, the agents know nothing.

63
00:04:18,095 --> 00:04:19,967
Now I scrub forward.

64
00:04:20,247 --> 00:04:26,463
Watch the arrows appear near the goal first, the values spread backwards from the goal, like a ripple.

65
00:04:26,743 --> 00:04:28,951
Green is good, red is bad.

66
00:04:29,231 --> 00:04:31,679
At the end, look at the blue paths.

67
00:04:31,959 --> 00:04:35,727
Q-learning goes along the edge of the cliff, the shortest route.

68
00:04:36,007 --> 00:04:40,135
SARSA goes up and around, longer but away from the edge.

69
00:04:40,415 --> 00:04:44,927
In my run, Q-learning's path was fourteen cells and SARSA's was eighteen.

70
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,463
Now scroll down to the reward chart.

71
00:04:47,743 --> 00:04:50,719
This is reward during learning, not after.

72
00:04:50,999 --> 00:04:53,591
SARSA's line sits above Q-learning's.

73
00:04:53,871 --> 00:05:04,191
I checked this headlessly across ten random seeds, averaging the last fifth of a thousand episodes: SARSA averaged around twenty-seven, Q-learning around two.

74
00:05:04,471 --> 00:05:12,511
The little caption under each heatmap is noisier, because it only averages twenty episodes, so don't trust it for a single run.

75
00:05:12,791 --> 00:05:14,975
Here's the check that proves the idea.

76
00:05:15,255 --> 00:05:18,567
Set epsilon to zero, no random moves at all.

77
00:05:18,847 --> 00:05:23,119
Now both agents, when evaluated, walk the edge happily.

78
00:05:23,399 --> 00:05:26,783
In my test, both scored the same, thirty-eight.

79
00:05:27,063 --> 00:05:29,991
The cliff-edge path really is the best path.

80
00:05:30,271 --> 00:05:33,511
The difference only exists because of exploration.

81
00:05:34,311 --> 00:05:36,183
Two things surprised me.

82
00:05:36,463 --> 00:05:39,463
First, that Q-learning is not "worse".

83
00:05:39,743 --> 00:05:42,263
It learns the genuinely optimal path.

84
00:05:42,543 --> 00:05:45,519
If you turned off the random moves, it wins.

85
00:05:45,799 --> 00:05:49,687
It's just that while it's still exploring, it pays for that optimism.

86
00:05:49,967 --> 00:05:53,351
It's learning about a policy it isn't actually following.

87
00:05:53,631 --> 00:05:58,647
SARSA learns the value of the policy it is really running, clumsiness included.

88
00:05:58,927 --> 00:06:05,359
So when your behaviour is risky, SARSA says: given that I'll sometimes stumble, stay away from the edge.

89
00:06:05,639 --> 00:06:08,903
Second, this is a real lesson for real systems.

90
00:06:09,183 --> 00:06:19,551
If the thing you're training can't afford to fail while it learns, a robot, a trading bot, anything physical, the algorithm that accounts for its own mistakes might be the right choice.

91
00:06:20,351 --> 00:06:22,295
Let me be honest about the limits.

92
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:25,311
This is a tiny grid with a lookup table.

93
00:06:25,591 --> 00:06:27,727
It doesn't scale to big worlds.

94
00:06:28,007 --> 00:06:31,895
That's why modern RL uses neural networks instead of a table.

95
00:06:32,175 --> 00:06:35,031
And not every world works with the default settings.

96
00:06:35,311 --> 00:06:41,863
On the Lava maze, Q-learning reached the goal in my test, but SARSA did not in a thousand episodes.

97
00:06:42,143 --> 00:06:47,879
On Two doors, neither agent got through with the defaults; they just wandered until the step limit.

98
00:06:48,159 --> 00:06:52,551
I haven't tuned those, so you may need more episodes or a different epsilon.

99
00:06:52,831 --> 00:06:58,615
Also, one training run with one random seed is a single sample, so results wobble.

100
00:06:58,895 --> 00:07:07,271
And yes, Claude Code wrote most of this, and I checked the numbers by running the app's own functions, not by trusting the text in the info box.

101
00:07:08,071 --> 00:07:09,943
What would I build next?

102
00:07:10,223 --> 00:07:15,887
A deep Q-network, where the table is replaced by a neural net, so it can handle images.

103
00:07:16,167 --> 00:07:18,039
Or let you draw your own cliff.

104
00:07:18,319 --> 00:07:21,295
Or add a wind that randomly pushes the agent.

105
